# Line endings — one canonical form everywhere.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Without it, Git falls back to each clone's
# `core.autocrlf`. On Windows that is `true`, so the working copy is CRLF while
# the repository itself stores LF. The bytes on disk then differ from the bytes
# in the object store, and only on some machines — which is the worst shape a
# bug can have. It cost us real time twice:
#
#   * #258 — assertions that read `.github/workflows/*.yml` byte-for-byte passed
#     on every developer machine and on Linux CI, and went red only on the
#     `windows-latest` runner, because there and only there the file was CRLF.
#   * #227 — `prettier --check` failed on files nobody had touched, because
#     Prettier's default `endOfLine: "lf"` compared against a CRLF working copy.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF on the way into the repository; `eol=lf` pins the
# checkout to LF as well, overriding `core.autocrlf` on every platform. Together
# they mean the file on disk and the blob in the object store are the same bytes
# on Windows, macOS and Linux.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must never be CRLF: `/bin/sh` reads the trailing CR as part of
# the command and fails with errors like `\r: command not found`. Covered by the
# rule above, stated explicitly so a future Windows-flavoured exception cannot
# quietly swallow it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the one deliberate exception. cmd.exe is the only
# consumer and CRLF is its native convention. Stored as LF, checked out as CRLF.
# Safe for the class this file closes: Prettier has no parser for these, so they
# are never checked.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary files — never converted, never diffed as text. `text=auto` already
# detects these by content, but naming them makes the intent explicit and
# removes any chance of a heuristic misfire corrupting one.

# images
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.bmp  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary

# fonts
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.eot   binary

# archives
*.zip binary
*.gz  binary
*.tgz binary
*.7z  binary

# compiled / packaged output
*.exe   binary
*.dll   binary
*.node  binary
*.asar  binary
*.so    binary
*.dylib binary
*.pdf   binary
